Review follow-up (2026-07-30) — output caps stay per provider for now

A review flagged this commit as blocking before merge. Each claim was
checked against the vendors' own documentation. What held, what did not,
and what was decided.

CONFIRMED — o3 regresses.
o3 is still served: 200k context, 100k max output, retirement announced for
2026-12-11. It now receives 128k and OpenAI will reject the request. This
commit did introduce that regression.

NOT CONFIRMED — deepseek-reasoner.
The review cited a 65,536 cap on deepseek-reasoner. That model no longer
appears in DeepSeek's model-list endpoint nor on its pricing page; only
deepseek-v4-flash and deepseek-v4-pro are served, both capped at 384k. The
figure belongs to an earlier generation. No regression on DeepSeek.

WIDER THAN THE REVIEW SAID — the defect predates this commit.
gpt-4o is still actively maintained (three current snapshots) and caps
output at 16,384. At the previous value of 64k it was already being
rejected. One cap per provider is broken for any provider serving
heterogeneous models: this commit widened the blast radius, it did not
create it. Reverting to 64k would not have fixed the design.

THE PROPOSED FIX REFERENCES SOMETHING THAT DOES NOT EXIST.
The review said to compute the cap "using the limits already present in
the registry". There is no per-model output-cap registry in this codebase.
The nearest field is context length, a different quantity — gpt-4o has
128k of context for 16,384 of output, a factor of eight — and for most
providers it arrives from the vendor's API at runtime rather than being
stored locally. Fixing this properly means building that registry, not
wiring up an existing one.

DECISION (Kevin, 2026-07-30) — do not revert.
Dropping OpenAI back to 64k would cap today's frontier models in order to
protect o3: a maintained model, but one used by a very small minority. Not
a trade worth making. The raised caps stand as committed.

AGREED ARCHITECTURE — resolve the cap per model, not per provider.
`ProviderSpec::default_max_tokens` sits at the wrong level:
`stream_http::build_chat_payload` ships it for every model of that
provider. It should be resolved from the selected model. Two candidate
approaches:

  1. A per-model cap registry with a per-provider fallback. Explicit and
     testable, but one more table to keep current — the same maintenance
     trap we just walked away from for provider descriptions.

  2. Retry without the cap when the vendor rejects the request for that
     reason. Self-adjusting, needs no table, but it touches the streaming
     path and spends one failed round trip per unknown model.

Either approach also repairs gpt-4o, which the current design breaks.

ALSO OPEN — no automated coverage for the splash layout work (201d518).
The measurements behind that commit were real but ran in a throwaway
browser harness that was never versioned, so nothing would catch a future
regression. The only existing style test asserts a single CSS declaration
by regex: src/components/agent-import/__tests__/agent-import-styles.test.ts
